Confirmed empirically: the `api_key.revoke` event written by the pass lands with `row_hash = NULL` while every neighbouring event is chained.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "/Users/arijit/Coding/Python/DataQ/backend/app/services/offboarding_service.py",
    "line": 385,
    "summary": "`db.flush()` after the api_key.revoke `record_entity_change` calls moves those AuditEvent rows out of `session.new` before any commit, and the #1460 hash-chain hook (`audit_chain._chain_pending_audit_events`, a `before_commit` listener that scans `session.new` only) never sees them — they are inserted with NULL `prev_hash`/`row_hash`. Verified against the real test DB: after an offboard the chain is [api_key.mint True, suite.transfer True, api_key.revoke FALSE, workspace_member.remove True, user.offboard True]. The receipt (user.offboard) IS chained, but the privileged credential-revoke events it summarises are off-chain and `verify_chain` reports them as 'unverifiable legacy'.",
    "failure_scenario": "Admin offboards a user holding N live PATs. Every `api_key.revoke` event from the pass is written unhashed; an attacker with table write access can later alter or delete those rows (e.g. re-attribute the revoke, or hide that a key was revoked) and `verify_chain` stays green, since NULL-hash rows are counted as pre-chain legacy, never as a break. The existing test only asserts the action string is present, not `row_hash`. Fix: drop the `db.flush()` (nothing after it needs the ids) or move the flush before `record_entity_change`."
  },
  {
    "file": "/Users/arijit/Coding/Python/DataQ/backend/app/services/offboarding_service.py",
    "line": 323,
    "summary": "Lock ordering in `_run` is: departing user row FOR UPDATE (`_load_user(lock=True)`) → then `assert_admin_remains` locks ALL stored-admin `users` rows ordered by id → then `transfer_ownership` locks suite, then new-owner user, then previous owner. `membership_guard.assert_admin_remains`'s docstring relies on 'users ordered by id' as the anti-deadlock ordering, and this path violates it by taking one user lock first out of order. (The `lock=True` argument to `_is_last_admin` is also accepted and never used.)",
    "failure_scenario": "Two admins offboard two admin users X and Y concurrently (X.id < Y.id). Request A locks Y, request B locks X; A's `assert_admin_remains` scan then waits on X, B's scan waits on Y → Postgres deadlock detection kills one with `DeadlockDetected` (OperationalError → 500, not 409). Same cycle against the standalone `POST /admin/suites/{id}/transfer` with `keep_previous_owner_access=true` (its ordering is suite → target → previous owner=X, while offboard is X → suite): A holds X wants S, B holds S wants X. And against `PATCH /admin/users/{T}/role` where T is the chosen heir and X is an admin: offboard holds X wants T (transfer_ownership's target lock); set_user_role holds T then scans admins and wants X."
  },
  {
    "file": "/Users/arijit/Coding/Python/DataQ/backend/app/services/offboarding_service.py",
    "line": 233,
    "summary": "The read-only GET preview calls `_is_last_admin`, which unconditionally runs `assert_admin_remains` → `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` over every stored-admin `users` row plus their `workspace_members` rows. `get_db` never commits, so the GET holds those row locks for the whole request — including the `_owned_suite_rows` loop that follows (line 246), which runs `deletion_impact` = 7 queries per owned suite (a `get_suite` load + 6 COUNT(*)s, of which only 3 are used; the results count joins through `runs` on the largest table in the schema).",
    "failure_scenario": "An admin opens the offboard modal for a departing admin who owns many suites with large run/result history. While the preview counts results (seconds on a big `results` table), every concurrent `set_user_role`, `remove_member`, `transfer_ownership` (which FOR-UPDATEs the target user), another preview, and any other `assert_admin_remains` caller blocks behind a GET. Two previews of admin users are also deadlock-capable with an in-flight offboard (preview locks admins in id order; offboard locked its user first). A `SELECT` without FOR UPDATE (or using the guard's read-only branch) is what the preview needs; the counts should be a single grouped query rather than 7×N."
  },
  {
    "file": "/Users/arijit/Coding/Python/DataQ/backend/app/services/offboarding_service.py",
    "line": 391,
    "summary": "`membership_service.remove_member` is called with `settings` defaulted, so it resolves `get_settings()` itself, while `_membership` (which decided that `state == MEMBER`) also used `get_settings()` unless a `settings` override was passed. `preview`/`_membership` accept a `settings` parameter for tests, but `_run` never threads one through, so a test that injects settings into `preview` gets a different membership verdict than the pass — and in production a `WORKSPACE_ADMIN_EMAILS` allowlist entry makes `_membership` return ENV_LISTED and skip the row delete, leaving the `workspace_members` row in place although the endpoint reports 'not withdrawn' only via `skipped`.",
    "failure_scenario": "Departing user is listed in WORKSPACE_ADMIN_EMAILS (the documented bootstrap seed). The pass transfers suites and revokes credentials, then SKIPS membership removal (correct per ADR 0043) — but the user also keeps `users.role='admin'` and can re-authenticate via OIDC/OTP immediately, mint a new PAT and reclaim everything: the receipt says `membership_removed=false` with a note, but nothing forces the admin to read `skipped`, and the frontend receipt path must surface it prominently or the operator believes the person is gone. Verify `OffboardModal` renders `skipped` as a warning, not as a success footnote."
  },
  {
    "file": "/Users/arijit/Coding/Python/DataQ/backend/app/services/offboarding_service.py",
    "line": 354,
    "summary": "`transfer_ownership(inner, ...)` and `remove_member(inner, ...)` each end in `session.commit()`; with SQLAlchemy 2.0.52 and `join_transaction_mode=\"create_savepoint\"` that is a RELEASE SAVEPOINT (outer transaction untouched — verified semantics), and `expire_on_commit=True` expires `user`, the `suites` list and `key` rows loaded through `inner`. Each later `suite.id` / `user.email` / `user.id` access is a refresh SELECT inside a fresh savepoint — correct but silent: the departing user's row was locked FOR UPDATE at line 323, and after the first inner commit `user.role` is re-read from the DB. `actor` belongs to the OUTER session and is never expired until `db.expire_all()` at the end, so `actor.id` in `log.info` triggers a SELECT on a new outer transaction after commit.",
    "failure_scenario": "Not a data-loss path, but an unbounded-query one: for a user owning N suites, each `transfer_ownership` commit expires the whole inner identity map, and the loop then refreshes every remaining `Suite` (`suite.id`) plus `user` on each iteration — O(N²) refresh SELECTs on top of the 7×N preview counts; on the failure path the `except BaseException` correctly closes `inner` and rolls back `db`, so the atomicity claim holds. Cheapest fix: construct `inner` with `expire_on_commit=False` or capture `suite_ids = [s.id for s in suites]` before the loop."
  }
]
